WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:09.680
Now back to our database architecture and let's understand how the database executes views.

00:09.720 --> 00:14.000
So now let's say that the data engineer is creating a view called top n.

00:14.040 --> 00:16.560
So the query can be sent to the database engine.

00:16.560 --> 00:20.760
And once the database engine understands this is a view this is not a table.

00:20.760 --> 00:24.640
So now the database engine is going to go to the disk storage and to the catalog.

00:24.640 --> 00:32.640
And it will stores not only the metadata about the view, also the SQL that is responsible for the view.

00:32.640 --> 00:38.240
So it's going to take the SQL statements that you have defined in the create view and place it as well

00:38.240 --> 00:39.080
in the catalog.

00:39.080 --> 00:44.040
So if you compare it to the tables we have in the tables only metadata, but in the views we have both

00:44.040 --> 00:47.240
the metadata and as well the query of the view.

00:47.240 --> 00:53.280
And as well you can see that the database engine will not create a table in the users data.

00:53.280 --> 00:57.560
So there is no where data stored inside the disk or the cache.

00:57.560 --> 01:01.060
So the actual data, the physical data will not be stored anywhere.

01:01.060 --> 01:06.060
We are storing only metadata and the query inside the system catalog.

01:06.100 --> 01:08.580
So now we tell our data analyst okay, we have a new view.

01:08.580 --> 01:13.300
And the data analyst can go and write a query in order to retrieve the data from the view.

01:13.300 --> 01:16.860
So he can say and say select from the view and execute it.

01:16.860 --> 01:21.020
The database engine can take it and understand okay now we are talking about view.

01:21.020 --> 01:24.300
So the database first has to retrieve not the data.

01:24.460 --> 01:30.820
It can retrieve the query from the catalog in order to understand what do we have now to execute.

01:30.860 --> 01:34.340
Then the database can execute the query of the view first.

01:34.340 --> 01:38.580
And the data for this query comes from a physical table called orders.

01:38.580 --> 01:44.060
So now the database engine is querying the orders to retrieve the data so that we have a data for the

01:44.060 --> 01:44.700
end user.

01:44.700 --> 01:49.340
And then it's going to be executed and the result is going to be sent back to the data analyst.

01:49.340 --> 01:51.500
So as you can see there is like two queries.

01:51.540 --> 01:58.740
The SQL engine first has to execute the query from the view, and only after that the database engine

01:58.740 --> 02:01.960
can execute the query that comes from the user.

02:01.960 --> 02:04.720
So actually the data comes always from a physical table.

02:04.760 --> 02:08.320
But we are not providing the data analyst and access to the table.

02:08.320 --> 02:10.760
We are just providing an access to the view.

02:10.760 --> 02:14.760
So this can happen each time an end user selecting data from the view.

02:14.800 --> 02:20.600
Always the database engine can grab the query from the catalog, execute it first in order to get the

02:20.600 --> 02:23.560
data, and then execute what the end user wants.

02:23.600 --> 02:26.720
And now if the data engineer says no, let's go and drop the view.

02:26.840 --> 02:29.360
So she writes a query in order to drop the view.

02:29.360 --> 02:34.480
And the database engine can go to the system catalog and delete both the metadata and the query.

02:34.480 --> 02:39.120
So as you can see, if you are dropping a view, you are not losing the actual data.

02:39.120 --> 02:41.920
So there will be no user data lost at all.

02:41.920 --> 02:43.240
So don't worry about it.

02:43.280 --> 02:47.640
What you are losing is only the query and the metadata about your view.

02:47.640 --> 02:52.440
It's only if you drop a physical table like the orders you will lose your data.

02:52.440 --> 02:56.160
So dropping views is not that bad like dropping a database table.

02:56.160 --> 02:59.760
So this is how the database works with the views behind the scenes.
